WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.230
What's the difference between a human
and a puddle? Membranes. A membrane is

2
00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.500
like a protected envelope, and it does
two important things. One, it separates

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.330
the inside of the cell from the outside
environment, and two, it helps control

4
00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.590
what comes in and out of the cell.
Membranes are made out of phospholipids,

5
00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:21.430
molecules with hydrophilic and
hydrophobic parts. When many of these

6
00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.510
phospholipids get together, they make a
membrane. Sitting in the membrane are

7
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.290
several types of really cool proteins
that control how the cell works. We

8
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:33.950
have receptors, ion channels, and
structural proteins. Receptors sit in

9
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.340
the cell membrane, with their tails
inside and their arms outside. They

10
00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.630
connect the passing proteins and tell
the inside of the cell what is going on

11
00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:44.560
outside. They're kind of like our
senses, and there are many kinds. Ion

12
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.030
channels are little tunnels, letting
things like potassium, calcium and

13
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.070
sodium through the membrane that the
cell needs to survive. There are also

14
00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.910
structural proteins that help the cell
keep its shape, move around and connect

15
00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.800
to other cells. All these proteins live
within or on the cell membrane and are

16
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.190
free to move between the phospholipids
in order to get to where they need to

17
00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.680
be. Why would they need to move? Well,
some proteins on the membrane send out

18
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.980
signals to get what they need.
Sometimes the signal of a lonely

19
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.760
protein is too small, so they find
strength in numbers and move together

20
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.350
on the membrane to have a louder call.
All living things have cells. Some

21
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.850
living things are made from one cell,
like bacteria, and some are made from

22
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.440
many cells, like animals. Although both
of these types of cells have membranes,

23
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.260
a big part of what makes them different
is on the inside. Just like we have

24
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.060
organs in our bodies, animal and plant
cells have organelles, small organs, in

25
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.050
theirs. And just like each of our
organs has its own role in keeping us

26
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.850
alive, each organelle has its own role
in keeping the cell alive. Each of

27
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.450
these tiny organelles also have
membranes that control what they let in

28
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.270
and out. For example, mitochondria grab
ions and move them through the membrane

29
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.740
to make energy to fuel the cell.
Another example is the nucleus where

30
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.740
the DNA is stored. Its membrane
protects the DNA from things that could

31
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.450
damage it. Membranes are important for
life and keep us from being puddles.

